SFCM_DATA_PART("audits/insights/insights.json",0,1,{"sample":false,"as_of":"2026-09-26","dashboard":"audits","intro":"Nineteen findings about what happens to the recommendations of San Francisco's three watchdogs: the Controller's City Services Auditor, the Board of Supervisors' Budget and Legislative Analyst and the Civil Grand Jury. They were chosen from about thirty candidates tested against 11,739 recommendation records, the follow-up reports and the report PDFs. Each was tested with at least three robustness checks; two are marked fragile because a check did not hold, and the card says which. Some groupings (kinds of recommendation, reasons for saying no, repeat findings) were assigned by an AI model (Claude) and spot-checked by an analyst. A separate script recomputes every number from the source tables and checks every quotation against the PDF. A status here is the last one recorded in a public follow-up, mostly reported by the departments themselves. A \"will not be implemented\" answer (called a refusal below, for short) is often a legitimate policy, legal or cost decision, and the cards say which reasons were given; the dashboard behind each card lets you check the underlying records.","insights":[{"id":"controller-recommendations-fell-three-quarters","rank":1,"theme":"trends","title":"Controller auditors issued a quarter as many recommendations in FY2021–25 as in FY2011–15","dek":"Recommendations in the City Services Auditor's follow-up program averaged 348 a year in FY2011–FY2015 and 85 in FY2021–FY2025 (−76%). The Controller's own annual counts show the same drop: 329 a year, then 78.","why_it_matters":"Fewer recommendations is not necessarily less oversight: assessments, Public Integrity reviews and emergency work after 2020 produce fewer formal recommendations, and auditors may now write broader ones. The drop does mean the Controller's follow-up program now tracks a much smaller set of recommendations than a decade ago, which matters when reading its implementation rates.","surprise":"Audits of Airport and Port tenants, once about 13 reports a year, have almost disappeared (1.8 a year), but the drop holds even without them (−70%).","numbers":[{"label":"CSA recommendations in follow-up scope per year, FY2011–FY2015 → FY2021–FY2025 (our extraction)","value":"348 → 85 (−76%)","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=csa, in_csa_followup_scope, fiscal year of date_issued"},{"label":"Same, from the Controller's own cumulative counts","value":"329 → 78 a year (−76%)","source":"CON-2495 p.3 (1,647 issued FY2011–FY2015); CON-2886 p.2 (2,496 through FY2020); CON-3533 p.2 (2,887 through FY2025)"},{"label":"CSA reports with at least one recommendation, per year","value":"43.6 → 16.8 (−61%)","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#source=csa"},{"label":"Airport and Port reports per year (mostly tenant and lease audits)","value":"12.6 → 1.8","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#depts contains AIR or PRT"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Recommendations the Controller's auditors track, by fiscal year issued","subtitle":"CSA audit and assessment recommendations in its follow-up program (issued from July 2010), count per fiscal year","x":{"label":"Fiscal year","values":[2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Recommendations","values":[404,328,375,334,301,182,264,242,133,109,122,75,127,66,33,128]}],"highlight":"2025","annotations":[{"x":2020,"label":"COVID-19; corruption cases"}],"note":"Source: CSA reports and follow-up reports, extracted by this project; the Controller's own annual counts (CON-2495, CON-2886, CON-3533) agree. Fiscal years run July to June. City Performance, Whistleblower and Public Integrity reports are outside the follow-up program."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["The Controller's own cumulative counts give the same drop: 329 a year in FY2011–FY2015, 78 in FY2021–FY2025 (−76%).","Counting every CSA recommendation, including City Performance and Public Integrity reports: 455 → 133 a year (−71%).","Excluding Airport and Port reports, which include many tenant audits: 264 → 79 a year (−70%).","Counting reports instead of recommendations: 43.6 → 16.8 reports a year (−61%).","Held despite a rebound: FY2026 rose to 128 from 33 in FY2025; the five-year averages smooth single years."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Recommendation counts depend on how auditors split their advice; one recommendation today may cover what three did in 2012. Our fiscal-year counts differ from the Controller's in some years because we split lettered sub-parts and date reports by issue date."},"caveats":["Counts recommendations, not audit hours or findings.","FY2026 (July 2025 to June 2026) had 128 recommendations, above the FY2021–25 average; a single year is not a trend."],"explore":{"view":"au-overview","params":{"source":"csa","years":"2010-2026"},"label":"See the Controller's recommendations by year"},"departments":["CON"],"tags":["audit volume","Controller","oversight capacity"]},{"id":"controller-implemented-rate-rests-on-report-closure","rank":2,"theme":"verification","title":"83% of CSA recommendations recorded implemented get that status at the report level","dek":"Of 3,385 CSA recommendations with a recorded status, 96% are implemented. But 2,026 of the 3,244 \"implemented\" come from a follow-up that closed the whole report and 651 from a report's absence from the two-year open list. Where the status rests on evidence about the recommendation itself, 80% are implemented (n = 708).","why_it_matters":"The Controller's first annual report put it plainly: \"Although departments report having implemented 96 percent of the 689 recommendations issued in that period\" (CON-1772 p.5). When auditors went into the field (48 memos, 644 recommendations), they confirmed 76% as implemented, found 15% partial or not done and closed 9% as no longer applicable. Field checks are targeted, so this is not a random test of self-reports, but it adds auditor-verified evidence to a rate the Controller itself describes as department-reported.","surprise":"In our data, closing a whole report records every recommendation in it as implemented, including 54 of the 58 disagreed-with recommendations recorded implemented. CSA may close a report when an item is resolved another way, so the label can reflect our coding rather than the Controller's judgment of each item.","numbers":[{"label":"CSA recommendations implemented, of those with a status","value":"3,244 of 3,385 (96%)","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=csa, status"},{"label":"Share of \"implemented\" from whole-report closure or absence from the two-year list","value":"83%","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#status_basis"},{"label":"Implemented, when the status is about the recommendation itself","value":"80% of 708","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#status_basis not report-level"},{"label":"Field follow-up memos: implemented / partial or not / no longer applicable","value":"76% / 15% / 9% of 644","source":"audits/data/work/status_items.json#status_doc_type=csa_field_followup"},{"label":"Same memos read independently by Claude Opus 5.5","value":"77% implemented (44 memos with totals)","source":"audits/data/insights_work/field_followups.json (Claude Opus 5.5 reading of each field follow-up memo)"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Share recorded implemented depends on the kind of evidence","subtitle":"CSA recommendations with a recorded status, by the basis of that status; % implemented (n at right)","x":{"label":"Basis of status","values":["Whole report closed by a CSA follow-up","Absent from CSA's two-year open list","Auditor field follow-up","Public Integrity status update","CSA follow-up summary"]},"y":{"label":"Implemented (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Implemented","values":[100.0,100.0,85.4,87.83,47.87],"n":[2026,651,493,115,94]}],"highlight":"Auditor field follow-up","note":"Report closure and absence from the Controller's list of recommendations open over two years mark every recommendation of a report implemented by construction (the second is our inference). Field follow-ups are memos in which auditors check documents. Follow-up summaries may list mainly items still open, which lowers their rate. The 76% field figure counts all memo items; 85% counts the 493 linked to a specific recommendation. Statuses are the last recorded in a public follow-up; most are reported by the departments themselves."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Field follow-ups alone, linked to recommendations: 85% implemented (n = 493); only 13% of all \"implemented\" statuses rest on one.","An independent Claude Opus reading of the memos gives 77% implemented across 44 memos, against 76% from the pipeline's extraction.","Self-reports held where tested: in 6 memos that checked only what the department had reported implemented, auditors confirmed 44 of 46 and found 0 undone.","The report-closure artifact is visible directly: 54 of 58 \"implemented\" recommendations the department had disagreed with were closed with their report."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Field follow-ups are not a random sample: auditors may pick audits with doubtful progress, which would make the field rate too low. Report closure can be correct; CSA closes a report when every item is resolved, including by acceptable alternatives."},"caveats":["\"No longer applicable\" closures count as not implemented in the memo shares.","Covers CSA audits issued from July 2010."],"explore":{"view":"au-status","params":{"source":"csa"},"label":"See the Controller's recommendations by status"},"departments":["CON"],"tags":["self-reporting","verification","follow-up"]},{"id":"grand-jury-law-changes-rarely-done","rank":3,"theme":"refusals","title":"Grand Jury asks to change a law or governance were implemented 25% of the time; fixes 49%","dek":"Of 3,416 Grand Jury recommendation-respondent pairs with a recorded status, those asking for an ordinance, charter change or restructuring were recorded implemented 25% of the time and answered \"will not be implemented\" 65%; asks to fix a control or procedure were recorded implemented 49%, and training 63%. Asks that need new money fared about like the rest (41% vs 44%).","why_it_matters":"Changing a law or who holds authority is the Board of Supervisors' and the voters' decision, not a department's, so refusals here are often a statement about who decides rather than a rejection of the idea. Operational asks that a department can act on alone are recorded implemented about twice as often.","surprise":"Recommendations flagged as needing new spending were recorded implemented nearly as often as the rest. And what the jury asks for explains little of its gap with the Controller: reweighted to the Controller's mix of asks, the jury's rate would be 46%, barely above its actual 43% and still well below the Controller's recommendation-level 80%.","numbers":[{"label":"Implemented: change a law, charter or governance (n = 430)","value":"25%","source":"audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation); audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, status"},{"label":"Implemented: fix a control or procedure (n = 840)","value":"49%","source":"audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation); audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, status"},{"label":"Implemented: needs new money / does not (model flag; n = 748 flagged)","value":"41% / 44%","source":"audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation)#new_money"},{"label":"Share of asks that change a law or governance: Grand Jury vs Controller","value":"13% vs 2%","source":"audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation)"},{"label":"Grand Jury rate reweighted to the Controller's mix of asks","value":"46% (actual 43%)","source":"audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation); audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Structural asks get done least often","subtitle":"Grand Jury recommendations by the kind of action asked for; % of recommendation-respondent pairs recorded implemented (n at right)","x":{"label":"Kind of action","values":["Train staff or officials","Fix a control or procedure","Study or plan","Report or publish information","Add staff, funding or equipment","Build or upgrade an IT system","Write or update a policy","Change a law, charter or governance"]},"y":{"label":"Implemented (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Implemented","values":[63.3,48.81,46.58,43.98,42.38,42.17,40.12,24.88],"n":[109,840,541,407,571,230,162,430]}],"highlight":"Change a law, charter or governance","note":"Action type assigned by Claude Sonnet 5 from each recommendation's text (one primary type; rare types omitted). Grand Jury rows are one per recommendation and required respondent; statuses are the latest column of the Controller's tracking documents (Admin. Code 2.10), which copy the respondents' own answers."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Excluding the Board of Supervisors and the Mayor, who receive 49% of law-change asks: 32% implemented vs 51% for control fixes.","Only classifications with confidence of at least 0.8 (n = 548): 16% vs 45% for all other kinds.","Using the separate \"needs legislation\" flag instead of the action type: 23% vs 46% implemented (n = 367 flagged).","An analyst read 18 random classifications (law change, funding and control): 15 plausible, the rest debatable, none wrong."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Action types are model judgments from short recommendation texts, and many recommendations mix kinds. The status is the respondent's own answer, so a law that passed later for other reasons may be recorded as not implemented."},"caveats":["One row per recommendation and respondent; a recommendation sent to five respondents counts five times.","Tracking covers jury terms 2003-04 to 2024-25."],"explore":{"view":"au-status","params":{"source":"cgj"},"label":"See Grand Jury recommendations by status"},"departments":["BOS","MYR"],"tags":["Civil Grand Jury","legislation","kind of recommendation"]},{"id":"repeat-findings-follow-implemented-fixes","rank":4,"theme":"repeat-findings","title":"85% of repeat findings with a known history follow a fix recorded as implemented","dek":"1,137 recommendations repeat an earlier one at the same department. For the 599 whose earlier recommendation has a recorded status, 508 followed one marked implemented; only 69 followed a refusal.","why_it_matters":"A repeat after a recorded fix can mean the fix did not last, did not reach the cause, or addressed the original instance while the underlying risk recurred. The records cannot tell these apart, so a repeat is a reason to look at how follow-up items are closed, not a charge against any one department.","surprise":"Repeats mostly follow recommendations recorded as implemented, not ones a department declined, even for the Grand Jury, whose recommendations are less often recorded implemented overall (54% of repeats of Grand Jury recommendations vs a 43% base rate).","numbers":[{"label":"Repeat recommendations / with a known earlier status","value":"1,137 / 599","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#repeat_of; audits/data/site/repeats/clusters.json"},{"label":"Earlier recommendation implemented / will not","value":"508 / 69","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#status of repeat_of"},{"label":"Earlier recommendation has no follow-up status","value":"538","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Status of the earlier recommendation, for findings that were made again","subtitle":"Repeat recommendations by the recorded status of the recommendation they repeat (unknown statuses excluded)","x":{"label":"Earlier status","values":["Implemented","Will not be implemented","In progress","Open","Closed otherwise"]},"y":{"label":"Repeat recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Repeats","values":[508,69,15,4,3]}],"highlight":"Implemented","note":"A repeat is a recommendation on the same problem at the same department in at least two reports and years. Repeat clusters were grouped by an AI model (Claude Opus 5.5) and checked on samples: a second model agreed on 87% of 150 sampled pairs, and an analyst agreed on 37 of 40. Statuses are the last recorded in a public follow-up; most are reported by the departments themselves."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Links with confidence of at least 0.8 only: 87% (n = 328).","Excluding 24 clusters about tenants and garage operators, where each audit covers a different business: 81% (n = 475).","Controller repeating the Controller: 97% (n = 408), close to the Controller's 96% base rate, and 87% of those earlier statuses are report-level closures (see the card on report-level closures); repeats of Grand Jury recommendations: 54% (n = 168) vs a 43% base rate."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The Controller's high implemented rate makes \"follows an implemented fix\" likely by default; a repeat can also be a new instance of an old risk that the first fix did address."},"caveats":["Clusters are a reading aid; a repeat is a reason to check, not proof a fix failed."],"explore":{"view":"au-repeat","params":{},"label":"See every repeat finding"},"departments":["AIR","PRT","MTA"],"tags":["repeat findings","implemented","follow-up"]},{"id":"grand-jury-refusals-mostly-not-on-merits","rank":5,"theme":"refusals","title":"Only 16% of Grand Jury refusals dispute the merits; most cite authority or existing work","dek":"A model reading of all 1,283 Grand Jury \"will not be implemented\" answers finds 38% cite lack of authority or jurisdiction, 17% say it is already done or covered, 7% cite a legal or contract limit and 5% cite cost. 16% disagree on the merits.","why_it_matters":"Juries address many recommendations to several officials at once, and the law requires each to answer. Most refusals say \"not our authority\" or \"already covered\", and 11% say the respondent is taking a different approach. These can be accurate, or a way to sidestep a question; either way, better-targeted recommendations would make the answers easier to judge.","surprise":"Cost, staffing or funding was the primary reason in only 5% of refusals, the least common of the reasons (secondary reasons were not counted).","numbers":[{"label":"Refusals classified (Grand Jury)","value":"1,283","source":"audits/data/redteam/reasons.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one primary reason per refusal)#source=cgj"},{"label":"Authority + already done + legal + cost","value":"67%","source":"audits/data/redteam/reasons.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one primary reason per refusal)"},{"label":"Controller (CSA) refusals for comparison: no reason given / disagree on merits (CSA responses are brief matrix entries, often without a stated reason)","value":"45% / 30% of 102","source":"audits/data/redteam/reasons.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one primary reason per refusal)#source=csa"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Why respondents said \"will not be implemented\"","subtitle":"Grand Jury refusals by the primary reason given in the printed response, % of refusals (n at right)","x":{"label":"Reason","values":["Not our authority or jurisdiction","Already done or covered by existing process","Disagrees on the merits","Taking a different approach","Legal, Charter or contract constraint","No reason given","Cost, staffing or funding"]},"y":{"label":"Share of refusals (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Share","values":[37.8,17.07,16.29,10.6,6.94,6.16,5.14],"n":[485,219,209,136,89,79,66]}],"highlight":"Disagrees on the merits","note":"Reasons classified by Claude Sonnet 5 from the extracted response text, one primary reason each; shares are approximate."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Only classifications with confidence of at least 0.8 (n = 608): authority 62%, merits 4%.","Excluding the Board of Supervisors and the Mayor (n = 786): authority 47%, merits 14%, cost 5%.","Held for juries from 2016 on (n = 419): merits 16%; the mix shifts from authority (17%) to \"already done or covered\" (28%), and cost rises to 9%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The classification reads a short summary of each response, and many responses give several reasons; the primary one is a judgment call."},"caveats":["\"Not our authority\" answers can be accurate or can be a way to avoid a question; the data cannot tell which."],"explore":{"view":"au-agreement","params":{"source":"cgj"},"label":"See Grand Jury responses and outcomes"},"departments":["BOS","MYR","CON"],"tags":["refusals","reasons","Civil Grand Jury"]},{"id":"emergency-water-study-yes-funding-no","rank":6,"theme":"safety","title":"Firefighting-water report: plans were reported done; the funding commitments were declined","dek":"The 2019 Grand Jury found the high-pressure emergency water system \"does not cover large parts of Supervisorial Districts 1, 4, 7 and 11, roughly one-third of the City’s developed area\" (CGJ-2018-2019-a4a61434 p.44). Of its 10 recommendations, 7 were reported implemented by every respondent. The two that would have committed money on the jury's timeline were answered \"will not be implemented\": a financed plan to extend the system (4 of 5 respondents; the Board reported it done through a study) and a purchase of new hose tenders (3 of 3).","why_it_matters":"On R2 the Mayor's Office, SFPUC, Fire and the City Administrator gave the same answer: \"Committing to entirely funding a single program out of context and without regard for the trade-offs of that commitment would be out of step with the City’s longstanding and highly regarded capital planning process\" (CGJS-2018-19 p.1); on R4 the Mayor said \"Due to fiscal uncertainty, the City is unable to commit to purchasing 20 new PWSS hose tenders by June 2021.\" (CGJS-2018-19 p.1). Those are budget judgments made in the open. As of the last tracker (2022), extending high-pressure coverage to the western and southern districts ran through the capital plan and a possible bond.","surprise":"Plans, drills and studies were reported done; the refusals came only where the City would have had to commit money on the jury's timeline.","numbers":[{"label":"Recommendations reported implemented by every respondent","value":"7 of 10","source":"CGJ-2018-2019-a4a61434; CGJS-2018-19; audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#report=CGJ-2018-2019-a4a61434"},{"label":"R2 (financed plan to 2034): will not","value":"4 of 5 respondents","source":"CGJS-2018-19 p.1"},{"label":"R4 (new hose tenders by mid-2021): will not","value":"3 of 3 respondents","source":"CGJS-2018-19 p.1"}],"chart":{"type":"stacked","title":"Respondents' last recorded answer, by recommendation","subtitle":"\"Act Now Before It Is Too Late\" (2019): each respondent's latest status for R1 to R10, count of respondents","x":{"label":"Recommendation","values":["R1","R2","R3","R4","R5","R6","R7","R8","R9","R10"]},"y":{"label":"Respondents","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Implemented","values":[5,1,1,0,1,4,3,0,2,2]},{"name":"In progress","values":[0,0,0,0,0,0,0,3,0,0]},{"name":"Will not be implemented","values":[0,4,0,3,0,0,0,0,0,0]}],"highlight":"Will not be implemented","note":"R2: financed plan for a high-pressure system in the areas without one, by 2034. R4: buy new hose tenders. R8: analyze a separate bond (last answer 2022: \"Will Be Implemented in the Future\"). Source: Controller's tracker CGJS-2018-19; no later tracker for this term."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Checked in the tracker text: the refusal wording for R2 and R4 is on CGJS-2018-19 p.1, and the Board separately reported R2 implemented through a 2021 planning study and a BLA funding report.","Not typical of safety recommendations: across the 1,025 Grand Jury pairs the model flagged as life-safety or public-health, 46% were implemented vs 42% of others.","Still unresolved: R8, a separate bond, was last recorded as \"Will Be Implemented in the Future\" in 2022, after which tracking of this jury term stopped."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The tracker ends in 2022, and money did flow through the regular capital-plan route: the City's 2020 answer notes \"Now that the ESER 2020 bond has passed, the City can begin to plan with greater specificity.\" (CGJS-2018-19 p.2). The \"will not\" answers declined the jury's specific plan and timeline, not all funding."},"caveats":["Some hose tenders had been funded; the Mayor's response says funding for part of them was cut in the COVID-era budget."],"explore":{"view":"report","params":{"id":"CGJ-2018-2019-a4a61434"},"label":"See this report's recommendations and answers"},"departments":["FIR","PUC","MYR","ADM","BOS"],"tags":["earthquake","fire","capital planning"]},{"id":"board-and-mayor-give-most-refusals","rank":7,"theme":"refusals","title":"The Board and the Mayor give 42% of all Grand Jury \"will not be implemented\" answers","dek":"Of 1,658 tracked refusals, the Board of Supervisors gave 385 and the Mayor's Office (including MOHCD) 312; the two are named as respondents more often than anyone else. Without them, implemented answers outnumber refusals, 1,039 to 961; with them, refusals lead.","why_it_matters":"The Board is a legislature and a required respondent to most jury reports. Its most common single reason (28% of 267 Board refusals) is that it lacks authority over the matter, as in 2004-05: \"The Board supports the recommendation of the Civil Grand Jury, but does not have the operational authority to implement the recommendations.\" (CGJS-2004-05 p.1). Much of the Grand Jury's refusal count therefore comes from recommendations addressed to policymakers, whose answers reflect jurisdiction or policy choices rather than operational failure.","surprise":"The Board, not an operating department, answers \"will not be implemented\" most often: 74% of its tracked answers, against 44% for all other respondents except the Mayor.","numbers":[{"label":"Grand Jury refusals: Board / Mayor / all","value":"385 / 312 / 1,658","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, status=not_implementing, dept"},{"label":"Excluding Board and Mayor: implemented vs will not","value":"1,039 vs 961","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj"},{"label":"Recommendations where at least one respondent implemented","value":"58% of 1,617","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, grouped by report and rec_number"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Who answers \"will not be implemented\" to the Grand Jury","subtitle":"Refusals recorded as the latest status, top eight respondents; n at right = all of that respondent's tracked answers","x":{"label":"Respondent","values":["Board of Supervisors","Mayor (incl. MOHCD)","Controller","City Administrator","Police","Public Health","Emergency Management","Municipal Transportation Agency"]},"y":{"label":"Refusals","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Refusals","values":[385,312,133,85,67,66,65,60],"n":[519,703,215,188,173,132,139,130]}],"highlight":["Board of Supervisors","Mayor (incl. MOHCD)"],"note":"Grand Jury rows are one per recommendation and required respondent; statuses are the latest column of the Controller's tracking documents (Admin. Code 2.10), which copy the respondents' own answers."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Per distinct recommendation rather than per respondent: 58% had at least one respondent implement.","The Board's own reasons (n = 267): 28% say it lacks authority, the largest single reason.","Held for reports from 2012 on: the Board and Mayor give 40% of refusals, though the Board's own refusal rate fell from 95% before 2012 to 58%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Required-respondent lists vary by report, so the Board and Mayor answer more recommendations than anyone else; their share of refusals partly reflects volume."},"caveats":["A Board refusal can coexist with a department implementing the same recommendation."],"explore":{"view":"au-status","params":{"source":"cgj","dept":"BOS,MYR"},"label":"See the Board's and Mayor's Grand Jury answers"},"departments":["BOS","MYR"],"tags":["Board of Supervisors","Mayor","Civil Grand Jury"]},{"id":"tenant-reporting-most-repeated-finding","rank":8,"theme":"repeat-findings","title":"The most repeated finding: Airport tenants' late revenue reports, in 26 audits over 14 years","dek":"From 2005 to 2019, 26 Controller audits, mostly of different Airport tenants, found annual certified revenue reports late, missing or uncertified, and in many cases fines not imposed; 25 of 36 recommendations were recorded implemented. 8 of the ten most repeated findings citywide concern money the City collects from, or pays to, its tenants and operators.","why_it_matters":"The City relies on businesses at the Airport, the Port and City garages to report their own sales, on which part of their rent is based. Each recommendation was addressed to one tenant's audit, so closing it did not necessarily change practice elsewhere. The last audit in the chain asked the Airport to \"Develop procedures to ensure receipt of the required reports from the Tenant by the due date.\" (CON-2751 p.10).","surprise":"The finding recurred across tenants for 14 years even though most of its recommendations were recorded implemented.","numbers":[{"label":"Airport tenant-reporting chain","value":"26 reports, 36 recommendations, 2005–2019","source":"audits/data/site/repeats/clusters.json#C-0117"},{"label":"Recorded implemented","value":"25 of 36","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#repeat_cluster=C-0117"},{"label":"Tenant or operator clusters among the ten most repeated","value":"8","source":"audits/data/site/repeats/clusters.json"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"The ten most repeated audit findings","subtitle":"Repeat clusters by number of reports that made the finding; tenant and operator revenue findings highlighted","x":{"label":"Finding","values":["Airport: tenants' certified revenue reports late or missing","Airport: late fees on tenant payments not charged","Port: weak oversight of tenants' gross receipts","SFMTA: garage operators not reconciling parking cards","SFMTA: monthly parking late fees not collected","Rec & Park: park evaluations inconsistent","SFMTA: operator expenses reimbursed without support","Airport: tenants not keeping required records","Port: tenants excluding items from gross receipts","Rec & Park: park evaluations not used to deploy staff"]},"y":{"label":"Reports","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Reports","values":[26,20,17,11,11,11,9,9,9,9]}],"highlight":["Airport: tenants' certified revenue reports late or missing","Airport: late fees on tenant payments not charged","Port: weak oversight of tenants' gross receipts","SFMTA: garage operators not reconciling parking cards","SFMTA: monthly parking late fees not collected","SFMTA: operator expenses reimbursed without support","Airport: tenants not keeping required records","Port: tenants excluding items from gross receipts"],"note":"Repeat clusters were grouped by an AI model (Claude Opus 5.5) and checked on samples: a second model agreed on 87% of 150 sampled pairs, and an analyst agreed on 37 of 40. Rec & Park park evaluations are an annual City Performance program, repeated by design."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["The 26 are separate audit reports, mostly of different tenants, so no report is counted twice.","The chain ends in 2019, when Airport and Port tenant audits became much less frequent; its end is a record of audits, not proof the problem stopped.","Held beyond the Airport: the Port and SFMTA garages show parallel chains of 17 and 11 reports."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Tenant audits are chosen by risk, so the same finding at many tenants may say more about audit selection than about how common the problem is."},"caveats":["Clusters are a reading aid; a repeat is a reason to check, not proof a fix failed."],"explore":{"view":"au-repeat","params":{"dept":"AIR"},"label":"See Airport repeat findings"},"departments":["AIR","PRT","MTA"],"tags":["tenants","lease revenue","repeat findings"]},{"id":"further-analysis-usually-ends-in-no","rank":9,"theme":"follow-through","title":"\"Requires further analysis\" ended about as often in \"will not\" as in \"implemented\"","dek":"Of 352 Grand Jury answers of \"requires further analysis\" that the Controller later tracked, 158 ended as \"will not be implemented\" and 137 as implemented; 56 are still open or in progress. Answers of \"will be implemented\" were kept 390 times out of 563.","why_it_matters":"Penal Code 933.05 lets a respondent defer a Grand Jury recommendation for analysis, and a careful look can legitimately end in a reasoned no. The record shows these answers ended in either outcome at about the same rate, so a deferral is not a signal of eventual action; \"will be implemented\" answers, by contrast, were mostly kept.","surprise":"A deferral was not a likely yes: counted by respondent, 45% ended \"will not\" and 39% implemented; counted once per recommendation, the two outcomes tie.","numbers":[{"label":"\"Requires further analysis\" → will not / implemented","value":"158 / 137 of 352","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, cgj_response, status_basis=cgj_status_tracker"},{"label":"\"Will be implemented\" → implemented / will not","value":"390 / 106 of 563","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#cgj_response=will_be_implemented"},{"label":"\"Will be implemented\" later turned to \"will not\", excluding the Board of Supervisors","value":"61 of 479","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#dept!=BOS"}],"chart":{"type":"stacked","title":"What became of first answers that promised action or more study","subtitle":"Grand Jury recommendation-respondent pairs by first answer, final tracked status (% of each answer)","x":{"label":"First answer","values":["\"Will be implemented\"","\"Requires further analysis\""]},"y":{"label":"Share of answers (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Implemented","values":[69.27,38.92]},{"name":"In progress","values":[10.66,5.97]},{"name":"Open","values":[0.53,9.94]},{"name":"Will not be implemented","values":[18.83,44.89]},{"name":"Closed otherwise","values":[0.71,0.28]}],"highlight":"Will not be implemented","note":"Only answers with at least one later tracker column. Grand Jury rows are one per recommendation and required respondent; statuses are the latest column of the Controller's tracking documents (Admin. Code 2.10), which copy the respondents' own answers."},"robustness":{"verdict":"fragile","checks":["Excluding the Board of Supervisors and the Mayor: 106 will not vs 76 implemented (n = 209).","Jury reports from 2012 on: 97 will not vs 81 implemented (n = 230).","Did not hold per recommendation: counting each recommendation once (266), it is a tie, with 125 drawing at least one final \"will not\" and 124 at least one \"implemented\".","Reversals of \"will be implemented\" are rarer and concentrated: 45 of 106 are the Board, which often agreed by resolution and later said it lacks operational authority; without the Board, 61 of 479 reversed."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The tracker records answers, not outcomes; a respondent may say \"will not\" because another body did the work. First answers were mapped from printed labels to the four Penal Code categories, which is imperfect for letters that answer in prose."},"caveats":["Excludes answers that were never followed up in a tracker column."],"explore":{"view":"au-agreement","params":{"source":"cgj"},"label":"See how Grand Jury answers relate to outcomes"},"departments":["MYR","BOS"],"tags":["Civil Grand Jury","Penal Code 933.05","follow-up"]},{"id":"police-civilianization-17-years","rank":10,"theme":"repeat-findings","title":"Three watchdogs asked Police to civilianize desk jobs in 8 reports over 17 years","dek":"From the 2007 Grand Jury to the Budget and Legislative Analyst in 2024, 21 recommendations asked the Police Department to move sworn officers out of jobs civilians could do. In 2024 the BLA reported that \"all plans to move forward with civilianization stopped following the start of the COVID-19 pandemic and have not resumed\" (BLA-48c11f71 p.109).","why_it_matters":"Civilianization is meant to return sworn officers to duties that need them. SFPD has made progress: \"Since FY12-13, the SFPD has successfully civilianized 60 positions recommended in a variety of prior reports\" (CON-2711 p.2). SFPD's position, as the BLA summarized it, is that \"hiring civilians has little-to-no impact on sworn overtime costs\" (BLA-48c11f71 p.120), and SFPD says some positions need sworn expertise.","surprise":"SFPD has accepted the idea for specific positions: in 2018 \"SFPD has identified 25 positions for immediate civilianization as reflected in the FY 18‐ 19 Mayor's budget proposal.\" (BLA-165267bf p.91).","numbers":[{"label":"Reports in the repeat cluster (Grand Jury, Controller, BLA)","value":"8 reports, 21 recommendations, 2007–2024","source":"audits/data/site/repeats/clusters.json#C-0241"},{"label":"BLA 2024 on civilianization since FY2019-20","value":"\"SFPD was unable to share details on any positions that have been civilianized since FY 2019-20.\"","source":"BLA-48c11f71 p.109"},{"label":"Of the recommendations, recorded implemented / will not / no public follow-up","value":"2 / 1 / 18","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#repeat_cluster=C-0241"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Civilianization recommendations to the Police Department, by report","subtitle":"Number of recommendations in each report that ask to move sworn officers out of civilian-type jobs","x":{"label":"Report","values":["2007 Civil Grand Jury (CGJ-2006-2007-68313e86)","2008 Controller (CON-716)","2008 Controller (CON-761)","2008 Controller (CON-773)","2010 Controller (CON-1144)","2018 Budget and Legislative Analyst (BLA-165267bf)","2019 Controller (CON-2711)","2024 Budget and Legislative Analyst (BLA-48c11f71)"]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Recommendations","values":[3,1,6,5,1,1,3,1]}],"highlight":["2024 Budget and Legislative Analyst (BLA-48c11f71)"],"note":"The 2008 Controller reports (CON-761, CON-773) were prepared for the Controller by the Police Executive Research Forum. Repeat clusters were grouped by an AI model (Claude Opus 5.5) and checked on samples: a second model agreed on 87% of 150 sampled pairs, and an analyst agreed on 37 of 40. Cluster C-0241."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Each report in the chain was checked against its PDF, and the description of the period after 2020 was corrected to the BLA's exact wording.","The cluster spans all 3 recommending bodies, so it is not one auditor's concern.","Progress before 2020 is on record: the Controller's 2019 count and SFPD's 2018 list, both quoted above, show conversions, so the gap is about the period since."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The BLA says SFPD could not share details, not that no positions were civilianized; budget documents may show conversions the audits do not."},"caveats":["Grand Jury 2006-07 is a scanned report; its wording is not quoted here."],"explore":{"view":"au-repeat","params":{"dept":"POL"},"label":"See Police repeat findings"},"departments":["POL"],"tags":["civilianization","overtime","police staffing"]},{"id":"board-analyst-recommendations-untracked","rank":11,"theme":"follow-through","title":"None of the Board's budget analyst's 662 audit recommendations has a published status","dek":"The Board of Supervisors' analyst issued 662 recommendations in 37 performance audits from 2010 to 2026; departments agreed at least in part with 75%. None has a published status, against 51% of the Controller's and 76% of the Grand Jury's.","why_it_matters":"Unlike Grand Jury reports, which respondents must answer under state law, and Controller audits, which have a formal follow-up program, BLA audits carry no published tracking requirement. When a later audit looks back, the news can be mixed: the BLA's 2024 Police overtime audit found \"2018 BLA Audit Recommendations Were Not Implemented, Including Cost-Effectiveness Analysis\" (BLA-48c11f71 p.99), and that \"SFPD partially implemented two of these recommendations.\" (BLA-48c11f71 p.99). The Board commissions these audits; whether and how to track them is its decision.","surprise":"Departments agreed at least in part with most BLA recommendations, but no regular public record shows whether they carried them out.","numbers":[{"label":"BLA audit recommendations / reports","value":"662 / 37","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=bla"},{"label":"Agreed or partially agreed","value":"75%","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=bla, agreed"},{"label":"With a public follow-up status","value":"0","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=bla, status"},{"label":"Later recommendations that repeat a BLA recommendation","value":"40, repeating 26 BLA recommendations","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#repeat_of"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Share of recommendations with any public follow-up status","subtitle":"All recommendations extracted, by recommending body (n at right)","x":{"label":"Recommending body","values":["Controller's City Services Auditor","Civil Grand Jury","Budget and Legislative Analyst"]},"y":{"label":"With a status (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"With a status","values":[51.32,76.32,0.0],"n":[6596,4481,662]}],"highlight":"Budget and Legislative Analyst","note":"Controller follow-up covers audits issued from July 2010; Grand Jury tracking covers jury terms from 2003-04. The BLA publishes no follow-up series."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Later audits are the only public trace we found: 40 later recommendations repeat 26 BLA recommendations.","Held in the full BLA listing: of 241 documents, 2 have \"follow-up\" in the title and neither reports on audit recommendations; some later audits review earlier ones, as the 2024 Police audit did.","The comparison is conservative: the Controller's share counts older audits outside its follow-up program as untracked."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Departments may report progress to Board committees in hearings, which this project does not capture."},"caveats":["BLA reports without \"audit\" in the title were not extracted."],"explore":{"view":"au-status","params":{"source":"bla"},"label":"See BLA recommendations"},"departments":["BOS","POL"],"tags":["Budget and Legislative Analyst","follow-up","Board of Supervisors"]},{"id":"contractor-evaluations-30-years","rank":12,"theme":"repeat-findings","title":"Rating contractors' past work: urged in 1996, required in 2016, database unused in 2024","dek":"In 1996 the Grand Jury urged that \"Performance standards for contractors on city projects should be established and a formal review process instituted for each contractor and subcontractor.\" (CGJ-1995-1996-52783143 pp.2–3). A 2016 ordinance required it, yet in 2024 the BLA found the six construction departments \"are not using the City’s contractor performance and evaluation database, as required by section 6.26 of the City’s Administrative Code\" (BLA-f9cb966a p.10); in 2026 it again urged more use of past-performance tools (BLA-8208fb13 p.7).","why_it_matters":"Without shared ratings, a contractor with a poor record on one City project may win the next one at another department. The rule exists; the shared database is not used as required. Budget limits are one stated reason: the 2021-22 jury asked that \"the Mayor require all Chapter 6 departments to begin submitting evaluations into the database\", and in 2024 the Mayor answered \"will not be implemented\" because \"The Department of Public Works, similar to all City Departments, faces budget constraints and therefore cannot currently support adding a Manager position in the Department.\" (CGJS-2021-22 p.2).","surprise":"It was recorded as implemented: the Mayor reported the 2014-15 jury's recommendation done by an ordinance, noting that \"The ordinance requires Chapter 6 departments to document, evaluate, and report the performance of all contractors awarded construction contracts under Chapter 6\" (CGJS-2014-15 p.1).","numbers":[{"label":"Reports in the repeat cluster","value":"6 reports, 7 recommendations, 1996–2026 (30 years)","source":"audits/data/site/repeats/clusters.json#C-0137"},{"label":"Of those, recorded implemented / other status / no public follow-up","value":"3 / 1 / 3","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#repeat_cluster=C-0137"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Reports asking for contractor performance evaluations in public works","subtitle":"Recommendations per report on evaluating contractors and using past performance in awards","x":{"label":"Report","values":["1996 Civil Grand Jury (CGJ-1995-1996-52783143)","2013 Controller (CON-1604)","2014 Controller (CON-1747)","2014 Controller (CON-1757)","2015 Civil Grand Jury (CGJ-2014-2015-a4a75d07)","2026 Budget and Legislative Analyst (BLA-8208fb13)"]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Recommendations","values":[1,2,1,1,1,1]}],"highlight":["2026 Budget and Legislative Analyst (BLA-8208fb13)"],"note":"Repeat clusters were grouped by an AI model (Claude Opus 5.5) and checked on samples: a second model agreed on 87% of 150 sampled pairs, and an analyst agreed on 37 of 40. Cluster C-0137 (Public Works). The 2024 BLA finding (BLA-f9cb966a) covers all six Chapter 6 departments. Statuses as recorded."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Each link in the chain was checked against the PDFs, which corrected an earlier attribution: on the 2014-15 recommendation only the Board said \"will not\"; the Mayor reported the ordinance.","The requirement is in law (Administrative Code section 6.26), so \"not in routine use\" is measured against a written duty, per the BLA.","Held across sources: the Grand Jury, the Controller and the BLA each made the recommendation."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"A database pilot ran in FY2018-19 and departments may keep their own evaluations; the BLA finding is about the shared database."},"caveats":["The 1995-96 report does not number its recommendations."],"explore":{"view":"au-repeat","params":{"dept":"DPW"},"label":"See Public Works repeat findings"},"departments":["DPW","PUC","MTA","REC","PRT","AIR"],"tags":["contracting","Chapter 6","construction"]},{"id":"open-recommendations-not-updated","rank":13,"theme":"follow-through","title":"73% of recommendations recorded open over two years have had no public update in two","dek":"223 recommendations issued more than two years ago are still recorded as open or in progress; 163 of them have had no public follow-up in two years, and 144 in five. The median last update was in 2019.","why_it_matters":"\"Open\" in these records often means \"last heard of\", not \"still being worked on\". Most are Grand Jury items whose public tracking ended after a few annual updates, as the tracking process is designed. The public record cannot tell whether these were done, dropped or overtaken.","surprise":"The list of long-open items is mostly a list of items with no recent public follow-up.","numbers":[{"label":"Open or in progress, issued more than two years ago","value":"223 (Grand Jury 182, Controller 41)","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#status, date_issued"},{"label":"No follow-up in two years / five years","value":"163 / 144","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#last_followup"},{"label":"Grand Jury / Controller items with no update in two years","value":"134 / 29","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"When long-open recommendations were last updated","subtitle":"Recommendations open or in progress and issued more than two years ago, by year of the latest public follow-up","x":{"label":"Year of last update","values":[2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Recommendations","values":[2,6,9,0,6,5,7,1,6,41,7,18,22,9,11,8,3,30,26,6]}],"highlight":"2016","note":"As of September 26, 2026. Statuses are the last recorded in a public follow-up; most are reported by the departments themselves."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Using five years without an update: 144 of 223.","Held for both bodies: Grand Jury 134 of 182, Controller 29 of 41.","Held for items issued before 2020: 147 of 147 have had no update in two years."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Some items may be tracked in documents this project did not find, such as department reports to commissions. The 41 Controller items include 10 outside the Controller's current follow-up program; within it, the Controller counted only 7 open over two years in 2025 (see the card on the Controller's long-open list)."},"caveats":["Last recorded status, not evidence the work is undone."],"explore":{"view":"au-open","params":{},"label":"See open recommendations"},"departments":["CON"],"tags":["stale status","tracking","open items"]},{"id":"controller-long-open-list-down-to-seven","rank":14,"theme":"follow-through","title":"7 Controller audit recommendations were open over two years in 2025, the fewest on record","dek":"The Controller's annual count of its recommendations still open two years after issue ranged from 15 to 37 between 2014 and 2024 and was 7 in 2025, the lowest in the series. 6 of the 7 are Public Health's, from the 2022 audit of City Option, the program that holds employers' health-care contributions under the Health Care Security Ordinance.","why_it_matters":"A short list is consistent with departments reporting most Controller recommendations closed within two years, though it may partly reflect the smaller number issued since 2020 (see the card on the Controller's falling recommendation count). Public Health says \"The department is working with other city agencies and stakeholders to manage the City Option program, which has been implementing significant policy and program changes.\" (CON-3533 p.2).","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"Open more than two years, June 30 of each year","value":"15–37 in 2014–2024; 7 in 2025","source":"CON-1772 p.3; CON-2213 p.5; CON-2339 p.5; CON-2495 p.4; CON-2625 p.2; CON-2744 p.2; CON-2886 p.2; CON-3000 p.2; CON-3145 p.2; CON-3296 p.2; CON-3422 p.2; CON-3533 p.2"},{"label":"City Option audit (CON-3045) recommendations open or in progress","value":"6 of 19","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#report=CON-3045"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Controller recommendations open more than two years","subtitle":"As stated in each annual \"Recommendations Not Implemented After More Than Two Years\" report, as of June 30","x":{"label":"Year","values":[2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Open over two years","values":[28,32,35,37,16,34,15,33,22,20,19,7]}],"highlight":"2025","note":"Counts read from the reports' own text (CON-1772 to CON-3533) and re-read by the verifier."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Every count is the report's own stated number, re-read from the PDF text by the verifier.","Our record-level data agree: 6 of the City Option audit's 19 recommendations are open or in progress.","Noisy year to year: the list rose again in 2019 and 2021 before falling, so single-year changes mean little."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The list covers only CSA audits in the Controller's follow-up program, not Grand Jury or BLA recommendations (see the card on long-open items with no update), and rests on departments' reported status."},"caveats":["Fewer recommendations issued means fewer that can age past two years."],"explore":{"view":"report","params":{"id":"CON-3045"},"label":"See the City Option audit"},"departments":["DPH","CON"],"tags":["open items","Public Health","City Option"]},{"id":"port-audited-20-times-fire-per-dollar","rank":15,"theme":"coverage","title":"Per dollar spent, the Port drew 7 to 20 times as many Controller reports as Fire","dek":"In FY2011–FY2025 the Controller issued 37 reports with recommendations for the Port, which spent 1.9 billion dollars, and 6 for the Fire Department, which spent 6.1 billion: 19.6 versus 1.0 reports per billion. Fire had the fewest per dollar of the 18 departments that spent at least 1.5 billion.","why_it_matters":"Much of the difference is lease and concession audits, which protect revenue at the Port and Airport; spending does not capture that revenue risk. Fire's budget is mostly salaries, which citywide payroll audits may cover without naming the department. Over the same years Fire had 10 Grand Jury and BLA reports, against 6 from the Controller. The Controller sets its audit plan by risk; whether spending-heavy departments like Fire get enough coverage is a fair question for that plan.","surprise":"The Airport, which spent less than the Public Utilities Commission, had more Controller reports (73) than any other department.","numbers":[{"label":"Port: reports / spending FY2011–FY2025","value":"37 / 1.9 billion dollars (23 financial or compliance audits)","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#source=csa, depts; performance/data/site/spending/cells.json (DataSF bpnb-jwfb actuals, nominal)"},{"label":"Fire: reports / spending","value":"6 / 6.1 billion dollars","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json; performance/data/site/spending/cells.json (DataSF bpnb-jwfb actuals, nominal)"},{"label":"Airport: Controller reports FY2011–FY2025 (most of any department)","value":"73","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#source=csa, depts"},{"label":"Median reports per billion, departments over the threshold","value":"2.9","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json; performance/data/site/spending/cells.json (DataSF bpnb-jwfb actuals, nominal)"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Controller reports per billion dollars of spending, FY2011–FY2025","subtitle":"CSA reports with at least one recommendation naming the department, per billion dollars of actual spending (nominal); departments spending at least 1.5 billion dollars over the period; n = reports","x":{"label":"Department","values":["Fire Department","Public Utilities Commission","Public Health","Police","Children, Youth and Families","Sheriff","Homelessness and Supportive Housing","Human Services Agency","SFMTA","Airport","Public Works","Mayor (incl. MOHCD)","Technology","City Administrator","Public Library","Human Resources","Recreation and Park","Port"]},"y":{"label":"Reports per billion dollars","unit":"ratio"},"series":[{"name":"Reports per billion","values":[0.98,1.54,1.55,1.61,1.72,1.77,1.81,1.87,2.49,3.34,4.01,4.04,5.06,5.25,5.85,6.45,9.71,19.63],"n":[6,40,54,15,6,6,7,27,58,73,36,19,9,32,11,10,29,37]}],"highlight":["Port","Fire Department"],"note":"Spending: Spending and Revenue actuals (DataSF bpnb-jwfb) as exported by the performance module; nominal dollars summed over fiscal years. A report naming several departments counts for each."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Counting recommendations instead of reports: Port 67.4 vs Fire 9.3 per billion (median 19.2); Fire ranks 2 from the bottom.","Excluding financial and compliance audits (most tenant audits): Port 7.4 vs Fire 1.0; Fire ranks 2 from the bottom.","FY2016–FY2025 only: Port 11.7 vs Fire 1.1; Fire ranks 3 from the bottom.","Held with the Grand Jury and the BLA added, though Fire is less of an outlier: 21.8 vs 2.6 reports per billion (median 3.9); Fire ranks 4 from the bottom."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Reports without recommendations (such as clean audits) are not counted, and citywide audits that touch Fire without naming it are missed."},"caveats":["Nominal dollars; because all departments are compared over the same years, inflation affects them roughly alike."],"explore":{"view":"au-reports","params":{"source":"csa","dept":"FIR"},"label":"See Controller reports on the Fire Department"},"departments":["PRT","FIR","AIR","CON"],"tags":["audit coverage","audit plan","risk"]},{"id":"public-integrity-reforms-closed","rank":16,"theme":"trends","title":"After the 2020 corruption cases, 79 of 91 integrity reforms were reported fully done","dek":"The Controller's final update on its Public Integrity series (September 2026) reports 79 of 91 recommendations implemented and closed, 7 closed as partially implemented, 2 still in progress and 3 that will not be implemented; for one of those, the update says \"OCA and the City Administrator's Government Operations division considered the recommended rule change and are instead pursuing a solution leveraging existing reporting systems\" (CON-3624 p.19).","why_it_matters":"These reforms, on gifts, contracting, refuse rates and permitting, were a central part of the City's response to the Public Works corruption cases. The Controller notes that \"Closed recommendations indicate that the response provided by the departments described sufficient action to implement the recommendation or an acceptable alternative or a change occurred to make the recommendation no longer applicable or feasible.\" (CON-3624 p.4), and that \"The new Inspector General may evaluate the closed and open recommendations for follow-up to confirm the department’s reported implementation status.\" (CON-3624 p.4).","surprise":"\"Closed\" includes the 7 partial implementations (fully implemented is 87%), and no one outside the departments has yet verified the statuses.","numbers":[{"label":"Recommendations: fully / partially implemented and closed","value":"79 / 7 of 91","source":"CON-3624 p.3 (table total)"},{"label":"Will not be implemented / in progress","value":"3 / 2","source":"CON-3624 p.3"},{"label":"Public Integrity reports with extracted recommendations","value":"12","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#report_kind=public_integrity_review"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Status of the Public Integrity recommendations, September 2026","subtitle":"Recommendations in the Controller's Public Integrity assessments and audits, 2020–2025","x":{"label":"Status","values":["Implemented and closed","Partially implemented and closed","Will not be implemented","In progress"]},"y":{"label":"Recommendations","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Recommendations","values":[79,7,3,2]}],"highlight":"Partially implemented and closed","note":"Source: Controller, final Public Integrity status update, CON-3624 p.3 (September 16, 2026). Statuses are as the departments reported them."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["The counts are read from the report's own table and re-read by the verifier from the PDF text.","Our own count agrees in direction: 101 of the 115 recommendations we linked to these updates are recorded implemented (our count splits some lettered sub-parts).","The table records the 7 partial items as closed, not open; an earlier draft of our dashboard story had described them as still in progress."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The statuses are departments' own reports; the Inspector General has not yet verified them."},"caveats":["Individuals named in the source reports are not named here."],"explore":{"view":"au-reports","params":{"source":"csa","years":"2020-2026"},"label":"See the Controller's reports since 2020"},"departments":["CON","DPW","ETH","ADM"],"tags":["public integrity","corruption","reform"]},{"id":"largest-dollar-findings-mostly-estimates","rank":17,"theme":"dollars","title":"The ten largest audit dollar figures include one refund to ratepayers, cited twice","dek":"Of the 898 dollar findings in Controller and BLA reports, the largest are change orders across public works contracts in FY2022–24 ($187 million, 8% of contract value; change orders are a normal part of construction and the report does not call the amount improper), SFPUC bond-funded labor costs for which supporting documents were no longer available ($157 million) and a Recology settlement ($101 million) that appears in two reports. The rest are estimates, savings or cost growth.","why_it_matters":"Dollar figures in audits measure very different things, from money owed back to ratepayers to what might be saved. Adding them up would double count (the Recology settlement) and mix losses with forecasts, which is why this dashboard never sums them.","surprise":"The single biggest \"questioned\" amount is a records gap, not a finding of misuse: \"Except for SFPUC informing us that support was no longer available for $156.7 million of labor, benefits and overhead expenses incurred between June 1, 2003, and June 30, 2008\" (CON-3047 p.7), the audit found no unallowable spending.","numbers":[{"label":"Dollar findings kept after page verification (Controller and BLA)","value":"898 in 339 reports","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#dollar_findings"},{"label":"Recology refund","value":"\"Recology agreed to pay $101 million in restitution, interest, and penalties to ratepayers\"","source":"CON-3093 p.3"},{"label":"Change orders, all Chapter 6 departments, FY2022–24","value":"$186.8 million on $2.4 billion of contracts (8%)","source":"BLA-8208fb13 p.35 (table total row)"},{"label":"Refunds of overcharges among the top ten","value":"2 (the same refund)","source":"audits/data/site/reports/reports.json#CON-3093, CON-2951"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"The ten largest dollar figures in Controller and BLA reports","subtitle":"Millions of dollars, nominal, as stated in each report; kind in parentheses; never summed","x":{"label":"Finding","values":["Change orders across public works contracts, FY2022–24 (cost growth)","SFPUC bond-funded labor costs, 2003–08, supporting documents no longer available (unsupported)","Recology settlement paid to ratepayers, incl. interest and penalties (refund)","The same Recology settlement, earlier report: \"more than $100 million\" (refund)","SFMTA savings if costs matched peer transit systems (estimate)","Zoo management fee never adjusted for inflation since 1994 (estimate)","Rehab cost increases at two supportive-housing buildings (cost growth)","Central Subway forecast below its baseline budget (savings)","SFMTA engineering cost underestimates, three sample projects (estimate)","Recology rate over- and under-recoveries, per year (estimate)"]},"y":{"label":"Millions of dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"Amount","values":[186.79,156.7,101.0,100.0,88.55,78.3,69.71,64.46,60.5,59.94]}],"highlight":["Recology settlement paid to ratepayers, incl. interest and penalties (refund)","The same Recology settlement, earlier report: \"more than $100 million\" (refund)"],"note":"Each amount was re-checked against the cited page (08a_verify_dollars.py). Grand Jury estimates are excluded. Kind labels are this project's reading of the report."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Every amount in the chart appears on its cited page in the PDF text (checked by the verifier).","The two Recology figures describe one settlement: \"the City Attorney announced a more than $100 million refuse rate settlement with Recology\" (CON-2951 p.6).","With Grand Jury figures added the pattern holds: the Recology settlement is still the only refund among the largest; the largest jury figure, $750 million, is an estimated pension-liability increase, not a loss."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Kinds were assigned by the extraction model and checked by the analyst for the top ten only."},"caveats":["Nominal dollars as stated; amounts span 2007–2026 and are never added or compared across years."],"explore":{"view":"au-open","params":{},"label":"See recommendations with dollars at stake"},"departments":["DPW","PUC","MTA","HOM","REC"],"tags":["dollars","Recology","change orders"]},{"id":"grand-jury-told-already-done","rank":18,"theme":"refusals","title":"In 26% of first answers to the Grand Jury, respondents said \"has been implemented\"","dek":"In 898 of 3,480 recorded first answers (26%; one per recommendation and respondent), respondents said the recommendation had already been implemented. Another 219 refusals gave \"already done or covered by existing work\" as the main reason (as classified by an AI model). Together that is about 32% of answers.","why_it_matters":"Juries serve one year; departments answer from inside. A large \"already done\" share can mean juries recommend work already under way, departments began fixes during the jury's inquiry, or respondents count related work as the fix. The printed answers let readers judge case by case.","surprise":"Among the 10 respondents with at least 90 answers, Fire Department gave this answer most often (40%); the Board of Supervisors least (12%), as expected for a legislative body whose answers usually concern proposed laws or funding.","numbers":[{"label":"First answers \"has been implemented\"","value":"898 of 3,480","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=cgj, cgj_response"},{"label":"Refusals citing \"already done or existing process\"","value":"219","source":"audits/data/redteam/reasons.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one primary reason per refusal)"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Share of Grand Jury first answers saying \"has been implemented\", by report year","subtitle":"Recommendation-respondent pairs with a recorded first answer; % of answers","x":{"label":"Report years","values":["2003–09","2010–15","2016–20","2021–26"]},"y":{"label":"Answered \"has been implemented\" (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Has been implemented","values":[26.54,27.78,26.62,20.56]}],"note":"One row per recommendation and required respondent. Shares use the respondent's first recorded answer (the response letter or the tracker's original-response column), mapped to the Penal Code 933.05 categories. This differs from the final tracked status used on other cards."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held in every period: between 21% and 28%.","Excluding the Board of Supervisors, which rarely gives this answer: 28%.","Held across the 10 respondents with at least 90 answers; the highest is Fire Department at 40%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"First answers were mapped to Penal Code categories from printed labels; prose letters may be misread."},"caveats":["\"Has been implemented\" is the respondent's claim at the time of answering."],"explore":{"view":"au-agreement","params":{"source":"cgj"},"label":"See Grand Jury first answers"},"departments":["FIR","POL","DPH","BOS"],"tags":["Civil Grand Jury","responses","already implemented"]},{"id":"it-fixes-take-twice-as-long","rank":19,"theme":"follow-through","title":"Controller audits heavy on IT and staffing asks took longest to be recorded closed","dek":"Among 2,593 CSA recommendations recorded implemented with a date, the median time from report to the follow-up that recorded it done was 17 months for collecting money and 20 for control fixes, but 40 for IT systems and 44 for new staff or funding. Because most dates mark when a whole report closed, this compares audits more than individual fixes.","why_it_matters":"A report is recorded closed when its slowest item is done. Audits whose recommendations involve IT systems or new staff and funding were recorded closed later. Budget and procurement cycles are one plausible reason, but these data cannot separate that from how the follow-up program closes reports (see the card on report-level closures).","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"Median months, collect money / controls / IT / staffing","value":"17 / 20 / 40 / 44","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#months_to_close; audits/data/insights_work/rec_action_types.json (Claude Sonnet 5, one action type per recommendation)"},{"label":"Recommendations with a dated implementation","value":"2,593","source":"audits/data/site/recs/recs_*.json#source=csa, months_to_close"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Median months from report to implementation, by kind of fix","subtitle":"CSA recommendations recorded implemented with a date (upper bounds: most dates are the follow-up period in which a whole report closed); n at right","x":{"label":"Kind of action","values":["Collect or recover money","Fix a control or procedure","Write or update a policy","Report or publish information","Study or plan","Build or upgrade an IT system","Add staff, funding or equipment"]},"y":{"label":"Median months","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Median months","values":[16.6,19.7,20.9,25.35,31.8,40.1,43.8],"n":[216,1485,355,94,152,137,38]}],"highlight":"Build or upgrade an IT system","note":"Action type assigned by Claude Sonnet 5. Months run from report date to the first implemented event."},"robustness":{"verdict":"fragile","checks":["Recommendation-level evidence only (n = 567): IT 38 months vs controls 26; the gap narrows.","By the topic of the report instead of the kind of fix: IT 35 months vs revenue 16 and contracts 16.","Did not hold within reports: where one report has both IT and control recommendations (76 reports), their dates are identical 91% of the time, so the gap reflects which audits are IT-heavy, not item-by-item speed."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Most dates are when a whole report closed, which is set by its slowest item; an IT-heavy audit closes late even if its control items were done early."},"caveats":["For most recommendations the true completion date may be earlier than shown, because the date recorded is when the whole report closed.","651 inferred-implemented rows have no date and are excluded."],"explore":{"view":"au-time","params":{"source":"csa"},"label":"See how long Controller recommendations took"},"departments":["CON"],"tags":["time to implement","IT","follow-up"]}]});
